From 9d1e63b2ee49f7dbfc12ddb670832ed7c298f315 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:56:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: D2R's ground clutter tests on-screen first; lighting
 table blur 8-in-a-row only

VKD3D fex32 timed both 8-cell versions of D2R's compute shader
83e2eb4a6bf5f03b on the device: 8 cells in a row 2.2 ms per run, 4 x by
2 y 2.7 ms. The row version is now the only one.

D2R's ground clutter placement (compute shader 0e21a3b5088ba1af, once per
frame over a 1152 x 1152 grid, 1.0-1.2 ms on an Adreno 740) computes two
layers of density noise for every point that passes its coarse mask,
about 330 instructions, and only then tests whether the point is on
screen and whether a mask texture allows clutter there. FEX's version
moves those two tests ahead of the noise, each with its own early
return. Nothing before the old test has side effects (no stores, atomics,
barriers or subgroup operations; the called functions are pure) and the
combined test is unchanged, so every point that now returns early
returned at the old test without having done anything visible.

The version is an edit of vkd3d's own translation, not stored SPIR-V: a
64-word script copies runs of the translation's words in the new order
and adds the new instructions (two OpLogicalNot, selection merges,
branches, labels). It runs on the first translation with the expected
hash, and the result must have the expected hash too, or the game's
shader stays. With VKD3D_FEX_SHADER_SWAP=alt (default) binds take turns
between FEX's version and the game's own shader, so one session times
both; 1 = FEX's version, 2 = the game's own for this shader.

Pass meter: big clears (4M+ pixels) that get a render pass of their own
are followed to the next use of their image in the same command list
(cleared again, drawn into, barrier, nothing), with where the pass was
made and whether the image was drawn into before: a new 'gpu big clear
passes' line. D2R clears a 4096 x 4096 depth image in a pass of its own
every frame (0.55 ms).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01WZVGoVEaHJ78mpf2D3MiER
